# Esquire frameworks (tm) -- line-ending law.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The CI runner is Windows and its git has core.autocrlf=true, so git stores LF and hands
# the working tree CRLF. That is harmless for source that only ever gets compiled -- and fatal for a file that has
# to be EXECUTED inside a Linux container: the kernel reads the shebang literally, tries to exec "/bin/sh\r",
# cannot find it, and reports
#
#     exec /usr/local/bin/esq-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# -- which names the script, so it reads as "the file is missing" when the file is right there. The broker
# CrashLoopBackOff'd on exactly this: the dev tree was LF, the git blob was LF, and the CI CHECKOUT was CRLF.
#
# Declaring the endings here settles it at the source: every checkout, on every machine, whatever the local
# core.autocrlf says.

# Shell scripts are EXECUTED (container entrypoints, the OKE build/deploy scripts). LF, always.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are executed by cmd, which cannot resolve `call :label` in an LF-only file -- a lesson already
# learned the hard way on compose-rebuild.bat. CRLF, always.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
